--TEST--
Core\Arr::fill drops array_fill's start index, so PHP's array_fill(5, 3, …) is Core\Arr::fillKeys over the keys the caller actually wants
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
`array_fill($start_index, $count, $value)` takes three arguments where `Core\Arr::fill(uint $count, T $value)` takes two, and the missing one is deliberate: `array_fill(5, 3, "v")` produces the keys `5, 6, 7`, a list that does not start at zero — the shape `Core\Arr::isList` answers `false` for, and one every caller downstream then has to reason about. `nvs_core_arr_fill`'s doc comment owns that reasoning, and it names the two real uses of the parameter and where each went: a start of `0` is this member, and any other keys at all are `Core\Arr::fillKeys` over the keys the caller wanted, which is the first row below. The second divergence is at the other boundary and is not observable from a case at all: `array_fill(0, -1, "v")` throws a `ValueError` at run time, while `fill`'s `$count` is a `uint`, so a negative literal is refused by the checker before the program exists — the same trade `rule:types/declaration` makes everywhere, a run-time throw turned into a compile-time refusal. The agreeing half — every `array_fill` whose start index is zero — is asserted against PHP itself in `arr-fill-matches-array_fill-from-index-zero`.
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Render {
    public static function keys(array<mixed> $a): string {
        return Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::keys($a), ",");
    }
}

// What `array_fill(5, 3, "v")` is written as here. The keys are named rather
// than derived, which is the point: they are whatever the caller wanted and
// need not be consecutive, so this one spelling covers every start index and
// the strided and named cases `array_fill` cannot express at all.
array<int> $offset = [5, 6, 7];
echo Render::keys(Core\Arr::fillKeys($offset, "v")), "|", Core\Arr::count(Core\Arr::fillKeys($offset, "v")), "\n";

array<int|string> $scattered = [9, "name", 2];
echo Render::keys(Core\Arr::fillKeys($scattered, "v")), "\n";

// And `fill` itself, which has one shape: keys `0, 1, …` with no hole. PHP's
// answer at its own default start index is the same array, and that row is
// asserted against PHP in the matching case named above; what is asserted
// here is that this is the *only* shape `fill` has.
echo Render::keys(Core\Arr::fill(3, "v")), "|", Core\Arr::isList(Core\Arr::fill(3, "v")) as string, "\n";
--EXPECT--
5,6,7|3
9,name,2
0,1,2|1
